.\" mutos_c1.1 - manual page for the MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler back end
.\" Formatted with the standard man(7) macro set (.TH/.SH/.TP/...).
.\" View with: man ./mutos_c1.1   or   groff -man -Tascii mutos_c1.1 | less
.TH MUTOS_C1 1 "October 2026" "MUTOS 1700" "MUTOS 1700 Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mutos_c1 \- MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler, second pass (code generator)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_c1
.I temp1
.I temp2
.I assembly.s
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_c1
is the second of the two passes that make up the MUTOS 1700 cross C
compiler (the first is
.BR mutos_c0 (1)).
It reads the tagged intermediate-code stream written to
.I temp1
/
.I temp2
by
.BR mutos_c0 (1)
and writes
.BR mutos_as (1)-syntax
assembly text to
.IR assembly.s .
.PP
This is a from-scratch, modern-C11 reimplementation, byte-for-byte
verified against real MUTOS 1700 hardware output, not a literal port
of the original Version 7 Unix
.BR cc (1) 's
second pass; see
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
in the toolchain source tree for the confirmed
.IR temp1 / temp2
wire format and the exact opcode coverage this pass currently
implements.
.SH "CURRENT SCOPE"
.B mutos_c1
implements exactly the opcodes
.BR mutos_c0 (1)
can currently produce; the list is kept at the top of
.I src/mutos_cc/c1_gen.c
and, with the byte-level evidence for every instruction shape, in
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
in the toolchain source tree.
.PP
Every compiled function gets the fixed MUTOS 1700 C calling-convention
prologue
.RB ( "push bp" ,
.BR "mov bp,sp" ,
.BR "push di" ,
.BR "push si" )
and a tail jump to the shared
.B cret
epilogue (see
.IR docs/MUTOS_C_ABI.md ).
A local-variable frame of up to 80 bytes beyond the register-save area
is allocated with
.BR "sub sp,N" ,
one of 128 bytes or more with
.BR "mov ax,N / call chkstk" ;
the real threshold lies somewhere in between, so 81 to 127 bytes is an
explicit \(lqnot yet supported\(rq diagnostic rather than a guess.
.PP
There is no general register allocator: each operator uses the working
register the real compiler's output confirms (DI for most integer
arithmetic, AX/DX for multiply, divide and return values, SI as a
secondary index register, DI and SI for
.B register
variables, CX for shift counts and constant multipliers or divisors;
a
.B char
is loaded into AL and sign-extended by
.BR cbw ,
stored from DL with
.BR movb ,
read through a pointer variable via DX and BX, and compared with a
constant by
.B cmpb
or, compared with 0 when its address had to be computed first, loaded
into DL and tested with
.BR "orb dx,dx" ;
an element of a file-scope
.B char
array has the index in BX and the array's symbol as its displacement,
.BR _text(bx) ).
A local
.B static
variable is addressed by its label, a file-scope one by its symbol
.RB ( "mov di,_counter" );
file-scope storage is reserved with
.B .comm
or, for a
.B static
one, a labelled
.B .bss
block; an initialized
.B char
array is a labelled
.B .data
block of
.B .byte
values. A constant shift count of 1 or
2 is a repeated single-bit shift, a larger one is loaded into CX and
shifted by CL. A call's arguments are evaluated from the last to the
first, each pushed as soon as it is computed, as the real compiler does.
A
.B char
with one
.B int
operand is computed in AX (masked with a small constant, in DX), a
.B char
argument pushed from AX after
.BR cbw .
Registers are handed out in order: an address or a pointer goes into
DI, or into SI while DI still holds a value, a subscript index into DI,
SI or DX. A structure member is addressed with its offset as a
displacement
.RB ( "*2.(si)" );
a store through a pointer plus a non-zero offset computes its right-hand
side first, and a sum of several terms adds a plain variable after the
computed terms, both as the real compiler does. A bit-field is read with
.BR mov ,
.B sar
and
.B and
and assigned a constant with
.B and
(its complemented mask, in hexadecimal unless the value is 0) and
.BR or ;
a whole structure of 4 bytes is assigned like a
.BR long ,
one of 2 bytes like an
.BR int .
Floating-point code calls the software floating-point runtime in the
real
.IR libc.a ,
which works on a stack of doubles and takes an operand's address in AX:
.B "lea ax,<x>"
followed by
.B call flds
or
.B fldd
loads a value,
.BR fadds ,
.BR fsubs ,
.BR fmuls ,
.B fdivs
(and their double counterparts ending in
.BR d )
combine it with a memory operand,
.B fstsp
or
.B fstdp
store it,
.BR itof ,
.B ftoi
and
.B ftol
convert,
.B fcmp
and
.B sahf
compare (the operands exchanged first as the original compiler does)
before an ordinary signed branch,
.BR fadd ,
.BR fsub ,
.B fmul
and
.B fdiv
combine the top two stack entries,
.B fneg
negates the top, and
.B fsts
or
.B fstd
store without popping when an assignment's value is used; a
.B double
argument is stored into eight bytes of machine stack, and a function
returning a
.B double
leaves it in
.B fac
with its address in AX. Each floating constant, an
.B int
constant converted to floating included, becomes a
.B .float
in the data segment, or an 8-byte
.B .double
when it is not exactly a
.BR float ;
its text is the one the original compiler's own
.B printf
writes \(en
.IR libc.a 's
.B ecvt
of
.IR libc.a 's
.B atof
on the 56-bit software double, reproduced exactly \(en and a file with
floating code ends with
.BR ".globl fltused" .
A file-scope variable's initializer is written as the original
compiler's
.B doinit
writes it: a
.B .double
of the value for a
.BR double ,
a
.B .float
of the value truncated to a
.B float
(as
.IR libc.a 's
.B fstsp
stores it) for a
.BR float .
Every expression with a floating value is generated in the original
compiler's operand order, rebuilt from the whole expression tree (its
.BR degree ,
.B acommute
and operand-exchange rules, a floating constant that is exactly a
.B float
counting as a
.B float
operand, an 8-byte one as a
.BR double ).
File-scope, static, array (subscripted by a constant or a variable),
pointer and structure-member floating operands, compound assignments,
comparisons as values and under
.BR && / || ,
.BR long / char
conversions
.RB ( ltof )
and
.BR float -returning
functions are covered; a computed right operand of a floating
.B +=
is computed first, while
.B \-=
and
.B /=
load their target first. A few shapes are inferred from the goldens'
rules (see the source's
.IR README.md ),
and the shapes no real-hardware golden has shown are refused. An
.B unsigned
value converted to floating after a computed
.BR * ,
.B /
or call is refused for good: the original compiler's own output there
names a register that does not exist. An integer
.B "+ 0"
is dropped, as the original
.B acommute
does; a floating zero is an ordinary constant, a negated one written as
the zero, and a floating constant converted to an integer is converted
at run time. Of two
.B int
words read through computed addresses, a right one at offset 0 is
computed first, as the original compiler does: its value pushed for
.BR * ,
its address pushed and popped into BX for
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.BR & ,
.B |
and
.B ^
(and for
.B \-
with a variable on the left); of two compared, the left one's address
is pushed and the comparison made through BX.
A
.B long
combined with an
.B int
is computed with the
.B int
widened by
.B cwd
(an
.B unsigned
one by clearing the high word): added to the
.B long
from memory, compared in DX:AX \(en the high words signed, then the low
words unsigned, as the original compiler's long comparisons are \(en
or, as a constant, pushed and popped into BX and CX like a
.B long
constant.
An expression whose evaluation would overwrite a
register still holding a pending value \(en which the real compiler
handles by spilling or reordering in ways not yet reverse-engineered
\(en is refused by a built-in register-occupancy check, never emitted
with a clobbered value.
.PP
Any
.I temp1
opcode or operand shape outside the confirmed scope is reported,
never silently misassembled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I temp1
Input path for the primary intermediate-code stream, as written by
.BR mutos_c0 (1).
.TP
.I temp2
Input path for the string-literal side stream, read after
.I temp1
and rendered as
.B .byte
lines after the code's
.BR .data .
.TP
.I assembly.s
Output path for the generated
.BR mutos_as (1)-syntax
assembly text.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A malformed or unsupported
.I temp1
/
.I temp2
stream (an unrecognized opcode, a missing tag-byte marker, a
truncated stream, or an opcode this pass does not yet implement) is
reported to standard error and causes a non-zero exit status
immediately \(en
.B mutos_c1
does not attempt to produce best-effort assembly output for input it
cannot fully handle (what it wrote before the refusal is left in the
output file, unfinished).
.SH BUGS
.B mutos_cc,
the driver that would normally chain
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_c0 (1),
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_as (1),
and
.BR mutos_ld (1)
together into a single pipeline, does not exist yet; invoke this pass
directly (see
.BR SYNOPSIS ),
or see
.I tests/mutos_cc/run_goldens.sh
for a scripted example.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mutos_c0 (1),
.BR mutos_as (1),
.BR mutos_ld (1)
